course: history
lesson: 68
title: Rules, courts, police, taxes and armies through time
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ivy's strategy game has armies ready at the click of a button, and Leo thinks jails, police and income tax have always been there. Mia reads about trials with hot iron and calls them «so mid», slang for rubbish or so-so.
explain: What really happened: for centuries most soldiers were farmers who fought between harvests, modern police are only about 200 years old, prison as the main punishment spread in the 1800s, and Britain's income tax started in 1799 to pay for war.
explain: Think of a playground argument: you can toss a coin, or you can ask witnesses what they saw. Old trials by ordeal were like the coin, asking God to show the truth; witnesses and juries of ordinary people were a fairer way, and they slowly took over.
explain: Taxes shape how people live. From 1696 to 1851 England taxed houses by their windows, so people bricked some up, and you can still see the dark patches today. Oliver's nan calls police «bobbies» after Robert Peel, who set up London's police.
explain: Even war has rules. Egypt and the Hittites signed a peace treaty about 3,300 years ago, and since 1864 countries have agreed to protect wounded soldiers under the Red Cross; these rules are often broken, but they still save lives.
fact: In medieval Europe, trial by ordeal asked God to show the truth, for example by checking if a burn healed; in 1215 the Church told priests to stop taking part, and it faded away.
fact: After 1215, juries of ordinary people, usually twelve, grew in England, so people were judged by their «peers», not only by the king's officials.
fact: In 1829 Robert Peel set up London's Metropolitan Police, and officers were nicknamed «bobbies» after him; before that, towns had watchmen.
fact: For much of history, punishments were fines, shaming, being sent away or physical punishment; early jails mostly held people waiting for trial, and prison as the main punishment spread in the 1800s.
fact: From 1696 to 1851 England taxed houses by the number of windows, and some people bricked up windows to pay less.
fact: Britain started income tax in 1799 to pay for wars against Napoleon; it was dropped and brought back several times and became permanent.
fact: For centuries many soldiers were farmers who fought between harvests; full-time armies grew in the 1600s and 1700s, and France called up whole populations in 1793.
fact: After the Battle of Kadesh, Egypt and the Hittites signed one of the oldest known peace treaties, around 1259 BCE; a copy hangs at the United Nations.
fact: Henri Dunant helped start the Red Cross in 1863, and in 1864 countries signed the first Geneva Convention to protect wounded soldiers.
fact: In 1086 William the Conqueror had England's land, people and animals counted in the Domesday Book, for taxes; «doom» then meant judgement, because the records were final.
fact: The idea that the accuser must prove the case comes from Roman law and is in the 1948 Universal Declaration of Human Rights: innocent until proven guilty.
exercise: What was trial by ordeal? | A test asking God to show the truth, like seeing if a burn healed | A trial where twelve ordinary jurors listened to witnesses | A trial shown on television so everyone could judge | A fine paid in silver coins instead of going to court
exercise: What happened to trial by ordeal after 1215? | The Church told priests to stop, and it faded away | It became the only kind of trial in England | It moved to America and is still used in some states | It was used in every school to find out who cheated
exercise: Who are the twelve people in an English court? | A jury of ordinary people | The king's twelve sons and nephews | Twelve police officers on duty | Twelve judges sent from Rome
exercise: Who set up London's Metropolitan Police in 1829? | Robert Peel | William the Conqueror | Henri Dunant of Geneva | Napoleon Bonaparte
exercise: Why are police called «bobbies»? | After Robert Peel, who set them up | Because they bob up and down when they walk | After a famous police dog called Bobby | Because of their round, bobbing hats
exercise: What did early jails mostly do? | Held people waiting for trial | Punish people for life for small crimes | Store the king's gold and silver | Train young soldiers for war
exercise: When did prison as the main punishment spread? | In the 1800s | Back in the Stone Age | In ancient Egypt | Only in about 2020
exercise: Why did some English people brick up windows? | To pay less window tax | To keep out dragons | Glass was banned | To make their houses lighter
exercise: Why did Britain start income tax in 1799? | To pay for wars against Napoleon | To build the first railway line | To buy cows for poor farmers | To pay for new schools for all children
exercise: Who were most soldiers for centuries? | Farmers who fought between harvests | Full-time paid soldiers who never farmed | Children taken from their schools | Only kings and their own sons
exercise: When did full-time armies grow in Europe? | In the 1600s and 1700s | In the Stone Age, with spears | In about 1990 | They never really did
exercise: Who signed one of the oldest known peace treaties, around 1259 BCE? | Egypt and the Hittites | Britain and France | Rome and China, on the Silk Road | Iceland and Norway
exercise: Who helped start the Red Cross in 1863? | Henri Dunant | Robert Peel, in London | George Washington | Edward Jenner, the doctor
exercise: What does the Geneva Convention protect? | Wounded soldiers and people who can no longer fight | Only the generals and officers of both armies | Only the side that is winning the war at the time | Only ships and sailors, but never soldiers on land
exercise: What was the Domesday Book? | A count of England's land, people and animals for taxes | A scary story about the end of the world, read in church | A book of spells kept by the king's wizards | A list of all the famous wars England had won
exercise: Where does «innocent until proven guilty» have old roots? | In Roman law | In trial by ordeal | In the window tax | In the potlatch
test: Mia says hot-iron trials are so mid. What replaced them? | Witnesses and juries, which were fairer | Tossing a coin to decide who was guilty | Fighting dragons to prove you were innocent | Nothing, they still happen in Britain today
test: Freddie asks who the twelve people on the court show are. What do they do? | Ordinary people judge the evidence together | They are the judge's own family and friends | They sing songs between the trials | They guard the prison and lock the doors
test: Oliver's nan calls police bobbies. Leo says police are ancient like pyramids. Fair? | Not quite; modern police are about 200 years old | Yes, the pharaohs had police cars and police stations | Yes, the police even came before farming did | No, police were only invented in about 2010
test: Leo thinks jails always existed as punishment. What would a medieval judge more likely give? | A fine, shaming or sending away | Ten long years in a stone prison | Community service, done online | A parking ticket for his horse
test: Tom's dad says «window tax, mate» about bricked-up windows. What does it show? | Taxes can change how people build and live | Windows were illegal | Bricks were cheaper than air | Builders forgot the windows
test: Josh's dad grumbles about income tax; Grandad says it was meant to be temporary. True? | Yes, it began in 1799 for war and stayed | No, it's as old as the pyramids of Egypt | No, it only started in 2020 | Yes, and it was ended for good in 1800
test: Ivy's game has armies always ready. What would happen in real life if all soldiers left at harvest time? | Crops would rot and people go hungry | Armies would get much stronger and bigger | Nothing at all would happen to anyone | Harvests would double, because soldiers are lazy
test: Aisha says the treaty at the UN is older than Jesus. Is that right? | Yes, it is from around 1259 BCE | No, it was signed in 1945 | No, it is from 1066 | Yes, it is 300,000 years old
test: «why's that red cross everywhere bruv?» asks Ben. What is the answer? | It marks help for wounded people that must be protected | It's just the logo of a big football club from Switzerland | It's a sign for a chain of shops that sell plasters | It means danger, so you should run away from it, bruv
test: Molly says Domesday Book sounds like a horror film. What did «doom» mean? | Judgement, because the records were final | Monsters and ghosts that haunted the land | The end of the world, which people feared | A kind of tax on doors and windows
test: Zara asks since when «innocent until proven guilty». What is fair? | Old roots in Roman law, though not always followed | Only since last year, when a new law was passed | Since the days of trial by ordeal and hot iron | It's not a real idea, just something on TV shows
exam: Identify the principal reason for the decline of trial by ordeal in medieval Europe after 1215. | The Church instructed priests to cease participation | Parliament abolished all courts of law in the kingdom | Juries were prohibited by a decree of the king | Trial by ordeal was proven always to be correct
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the window tax in England. | It was levied from 1696 to 1851 and led some owners to brick up windows | It was introduced by the Romans and abolished by William the Conqueror | It encouraged owners to add more windows to their houses | It was a tax upon glass bottles and drinking vessels
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises military service for much of history? | Many soldiers were agricultural workers serving between harvests | All soldiers were full-time professionals from the earliest times | Armies consisted chiefly of children under the age of twelve | Military service was entirely unknown before 1900
exam: Ascertain the purpose of the first Geneva Convention of 1864. | The protection of wounded soldiers | The establishment of income tax | The creation of the Metropolitan Police | The compilation of the Domesday Book
